	log->l_iclog_roundoff = BBSIZE;
Reason:
When the superblock does not define a log stripe unit (`sb_logsunit <= 1`), the code unconditionally sets `l_iclog_roundoff` to 512 bytes (`BBSIZE`), ignoring the actual log sector size (`sb_logsectsize`). This undersized roundoff causes misaligned log writes on filesystems with larger log sectors (e.g., 4096 bytes), leading to CRC failures and mount errors.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
if (xfs_has_logv2(mp) && mp->m_sb.sb_logsunit > 1)
	log->l_iclog_roundoff = mp->m_sb.sb_logsunit;
else if (mp->m_sb.sb_logsectsize > 0)
	log->l_iclog_roundoff = mp->m_sb.sb_logsectsize;
else
	log->l_iclog_roundoff = BBSIZE;
Reason:
The fix adds a new condition to use `sb_logsectsize` (the log sector size) when no log stripe unit is specified. This ensures that the roundoff factor matches the actual log sector size, preventing misalignment and torn writes. If `sb_logsectsize` is zero (unlikely for a valid superblock), it falls back to the original 512-byte default.

